            <ArticleTitle>Patterns of referral to the paediatric specialist clinic of a regional hospital: descriptive study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>24-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the patterns of referral to a paediatric specialist out-patient clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Descriptive study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Regional hospital, Hong Kong.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The 1985 referral letters of patients who were referred to the paediatric specialist clinic during 1998 were studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Common referral diagnoses according to sources of referral.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The common sources of referral were maternal and child health centres (34.7%), accident and emergency departments (26.9%), the Student Health Service (12.9%), private practitioners (10.5%), and general out-patient clinics (9.8%). The common referral diagnoses from maternal and child health centres (n=689) were growth problems (37. 7%), heart murmurs (16.8%), and neonatal jaundice (10.9%). Asthma or suspected asthma constituted the majority of referrals from accident and emergency departments (227/533; 42.6%). Deviations in growth (41. 4%), problems regarding puberty (13.7%), apparent heart problems (13. 3%), and nocturnal enuresis (11.7%) were the common referral diagnoses from the Student Health Service (n=256).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Studying referral patterns from various sources is helpful in organising medical services and identifying training needs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
